endobj

1295 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 360 0 R/E(The average walking speed was initially considered to be 5 km/h, a value commonly adopted for walks on flat terrain. However, as the study area presents significant topographical variations, it was necessary to incorporate the effects of relief into the accessibility calculations.)/Pg 38 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

1296 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 360 0 R/E(To do this, we used Tobler's Hiking Function \(Tobler\220s Hiking Function\), an empirical equation developed by Waldo Tobler \(1993\) that estimates walking speed based on the slope of the terrain:)/Pg 38 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

1297 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 360 0 R/E(Where:)/Pg 38 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

1298 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1798 0 R/E(v is the speed in km/h;)/Pg 38 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

1299 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1799 0 R/E(S is the slope \(in decimal form\), i.e., the inclination of the path;)/Pg 38 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

1300 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 360 0 R/E(The function shows that walking speed is maximized on gentle descents \(around -5%\) and decreases both on uphill and steep downhill slopes.)/Pg 38 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

1301 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 360 0 R/E(Despite its precision, the direct application of this formula requires specific scripts, greater computational capacity, and a deeper understanding of walking times, which we were unable to fully address. To make the analysis more accessible and replicable within the GIS environment, a simplified version of Tobler's function was adopted.)/Pg 38 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

1302 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 360 0 R/T(v = 6 ^{-3.5} \(s + 0.05\) )/E(v = 6 ^{-3.5} \(s + 0.05\) )/Pg 38 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

